46. So what did “As per above” actually complete?

This is the question on which the two views finally meet.

Under View One, “As per above” completed an excellent clinical synthesis.

It allowed care to proceed rationally.

Under View Two, “As per above” may have completed something different:

the conversion of unresolved relationships into a sufficiently stable information state for action.

That can be entirely legitimate.

But it means we must distinguish:

completed document

from

completed inquiry;

service destination

from

causal adjudication;

plausible category

from

demonstrated independence;

operational closure

from

epistemic closure.

The source's own architecture insists on keeping those levels separate.

---

47. The question the hospital still has to answer

The strongest question is not:

Why did you not diagnose dystonia?

The record already saw dystonic phenomenology.

It is not:

Why did you diagnose Parkinsonism?

Parkinsonism was strongly supported.

It is not:

Why did you consider neuropathy?

That was a legitimate differential possibility.

The hardest question is narrower:

What clinical evidence justified the change in status of the painful distal territory?

If the same lower limb could be neurologically informative enough to contribute to an atypical young-onset Parkinsonian phenotype, what made its painful manifestations sufficiently independent to support another explanatory track?

What was observed?

What was tested?

What discriminated?

What evidence pointed one way rather than merely being compatible with one way?

And if THE PROTEST occurred, what did that protest cause the institution to reconsider?

